               communicate comfort. Sometimes they purr while resting, nursing, or even
               recovering from stress.
             </p>
           </div>
         </div>

         <div class="example">
           <h3>
             <button type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cat-q2">
               <span class="question-text">Do cats really have nine lives?</span>
               <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
             </button>
           </h3>
           <div class="details" id="cat-q2">
             <p>
               Not literally. The saying comes from cats' reputation for surviving awkward
               landings and tricky situations. They are agile, but they still need safe
               homes and careful supervision.
             </p>
           </div>
         </div>

         <div class="example">
           <h3>
             <button type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cat-q3">
               <span class="question-text">Why do cats knead blankets and laps?</span>
               <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
             </button>
           </h3>
           <div class="details" id="cat-q3">
             <p>
               Kneading is a comforting behavior that starts in kittenhood. Adult cats may
               do it when they feel safe, relaxed, or affectionate. Soft surfaces and calm
               routines often make the habit more common.
             </p>
           </div>
         </div>

         <div class="example">
           <h3>
             <button type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cat-q4">
               <span class="question-text">How much sleep do cats need?</span>
               <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
             </button>
           </h3>
           <div class="details" id="cat-q4">
             <p>
               Many cats sleep 12 to 16 hours a day, and some sleep even more. That resting
               pattern supports their energy bursts, hunting instincts, and daily recovery.
             </p>
           </div>
         </div>

         <div class="example">
           <h3>
             <button type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cat-q5">
               <span class="question-text">Why do cats bring home toys or prey?</span>
               <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
             </button>
           </h3>
           <div class="details" id="cat-q5">
             <p>
               Cats may be showing hunting instinct, sharing with their people, or practicing
               a learned behavior. It is natural, though the safest option is to keep indoor
               play focused on toys and enrichment.
             </p>
           </div>
         </div>

         <div class="example">
           <h3>
             <button type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cat-q6">
               <span class="question-text">How can I make a cat feel at home?</span>
               <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
             </button>
           </h3>
           <div class="details" id="cat-q6">
             <p>
               Give them a quiet place to retreat, keep food, water, and litter in sensible
               locations, and let them approach on their own terms. Predictable routines and
               gentle play help most cats settle in quickly.
             </p>
             <ul>
               <li>Provide vertical space like shelves or a cat tree.</li>
               <li>Use a consistent feeding schedule.</li>
